{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/specs/2026-11-17-pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity_cache.go; sed -n '1,260p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go; sed -n '1,200p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_policy_baseline.go; sed -n '545,590p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/specs/2026-11-17-pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity_cache.go; sed -n '1,260p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go; sed -n '1,200p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_policy_baseline.go; sed -n '545,590p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go\"","aggregated_output":"---\nslug: pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-17\ncompleted_at:        # stamped on the transition to status: done\nsupersedes:          # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on: pose-federated-source-proof-is-computed-once-per-operation\nremediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\npriority:            # integer >= 0 (lower = higher priority); ordering preference, not a blocker\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: refactor\nsurface:             # optional: minimal | standard | full (default) — minimal drops Tasks and derivable report sections; `pose specs facts <slug>` derives them\ndelivers: capability:review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nTirar duas despesas fixas de cada verificação de bundle: a cópia profunda do grafo de integridade de entrega que o guard de contrato do plano de revisão pedia a cada chamada, e a varredura de todo o `/proc` que a sonda de grupo de processos fazia a cada verificação.\n\n### Business value\nÉ o follow-up F1 de `pose-federated-source-proof-is-computed-once-per-operation`. No perfil da primeira chamada `pose_closeout_state` sobre o harne8, `resolveReviewGovernanceGuard` pedia `GetDeliveryIntegrity`, que devolve uma cópia profunda do grafo em cache (cerca de 5,5 s de CPU em `copyDeliveryIntegrityGraph`) só para ler os change sets do escopo; e `reviewProcessGroupAvailable` lia todos os `/proc/<pid>/stat` da máquina a cada verificação para saber se o sistema permite observar o fim de um grupo de processos (cerca de 4,3 s), uma propriedade que não muda entre duas verificações de um fechamento.\n\n### Constraints\n- O guard continua dando os mesmos caminhos e a mesma revisão base, e nunca altera nem guarda o grafo em cache.\n- Uma sonda que falhou nunca é guardada; um sucesso vale por um minuto.\n\n### Non-goals\n- Mudar o grafo, o cache dele ou o que a sonda verifica.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: The review plan's governance guard shall read the delivery-integrity graph through the cache's in-place view, deriving its paths and base revision inside the view and keeping nothing the graph reaches.\n- R2: The guard shall give the same paths and base revision as a deep copy of the graph gives, and the retained graph shall be unchanged after it.\n- R3: The process-group availability probe shall keep a success for one minute within the process and shall never keep a failure.\n\n### Non-functional\n- No harne8, a primeira chamada de fechamento cai cerca de 10% (média de 63 s para 56,5 s em rodadas alternadas), com o mesmo veredito.\n\n### Security\n- O guard só lê o grafo; a sonda guarda apenas o instante de um sucesso.\n\n### Compatibility\n- Sem mudança de contrato, saída ou formato.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n- `pose/delivery_integrity.go`: `Store.viewDeliveryIntegrity`, sobre `viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr`.\n- `pose/review_policy_baseline.go`: o guard deriva caminhos e revisão dentro da view.\n- `pose/review_dispatch_unix.go`: a sonda guarda um sucesso por `processGroupProbeTTL`.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_in_place_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_policy_baseline.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\nComposto em produção por todo plano de revisão (o guard) e por toda verificação que declara a contenção do revisor (a sonda). Check permanente `review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place-integration`, evidenceClass `integration`, required: `go test ./internal/pose -run 'TestReviewGuardReadsTheIntegrityGraphInPlace|TestProcessGroupProbeKeepsASuccessForItsTTL|TestABMProtectedBaseline' -count=1`.\n\n### API/contract changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Data/storage changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Technical risks\n- Um sistema que perca o acesso a `/proc` no meio de um processo ainda declara a capacidade por até um minuto; a capacidade declarada é a de observar o término, e a execução de um revisor continua verificando o grupo de verdade.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Guard pela view do cache (R1, R2).\n- [x] Sonda com sucesso guardado (R3).\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Testes de equivalência, de grafo intacto e da sonda, com prova de reprovação; medição no harne8.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Date: 2026-11-17\n- Context: o follow-up F1 de `pose-federated-source-proof-is-computed-once-per-operation` mediu os custos fixos de cada prova distinta.\n- Options considered: (a) guard pela view sem cópia e sonda com sucesso guardado; (b) memo por operação do grafo copiado; (c) só a sonda.\n- Decision: (a).\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\n- Rationale: (b) ainda copiaria o grafo uma vez por produtor; a view já existe no cache para leitores; (c) sozinha mal aparece na medição.\n- Consequences: o guard não pode guardar nada do grafo além das strings que deriva.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nTeste de equivalência do guard contra uma cópia do grafo, no fixture que muda o contrato de revisão; teste do grafo retido intacto; teste da sonda com a verificação substituída; os testes `TestABMProtectedBaseline*` do guard; medição no harne8.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/pose ./internal/cli ./internal/mcpserver ./internal/scaffold -count=1`\n- Scope: pose-mcp\n- Expected: verde\n\n### Execution log\n- Date: 2026-11-17\n- Environment: Linux, Go 1.26, Git 2.56; clone do harne8 em cc7780b1 com o pose-dist em 7e46b1e e commit-graph com filtros; `closeout-check spec:harne8-abm-governed-execution`, alternando o binário anterior e o novo.\n- Notes: 64 s e 62 s com o anterior, 60 s e 53 s com o novo, terminais. O teste do grafo intacto reprova com um guard que escreve no grafo; o da sonda reprova sem o sucesso guardado.\n\n### Results summary\n- Successes: cerca de 10% a menos na primeira chamada, mesmo veredito.\n- Failures: nenhuma.\n- Warnings: a medição teve outra carga na máquina; os números são médias de duas rodadas.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestReviewGuardReadsTheIntegrityGraphInPlace check:review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place-integration\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestReviewGuardReadsTheIntegrityGraphInPlace\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestProcessGroupProbeKeepsASuccessForItsTTL\n\n### Known gaps\n- Nenhuma.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n\nNão concluída.\n\n### Residual risks\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n### Review\n\nA primeira tentativa da rodada 1 não chegou ao revisor: o `pose validate --strict` reprovou em `release-test-compile-windows` (`GOOS=windows go vet ./internal/pose`), porque o teste da sonda usava `processGroupVerify` e `processGroupProbe`, que só existem em `review_dispatch_unix.go`. O teste da sonda foi para `review_guard_probe_unix_test.go`, sob a mesma restrição `//go:build !windows`.\n\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n)\n\n// The delivery-integrity index is parsed once per distinct content, not once per\n// caller.\n//\n// In this repository the file is 4.5 MB and a single `pose check --strict` read and\n// unmarshalled it 528 times: 2.4 GB of JSON for one gate. Measured, the read costs\n// 0.44 ms and the parse 10.7 ms, so the parse is 96% of it and the file is the only\n// input — identical bytes cannot produce a different graph.\n//\n// The cache key is the raw bytes themselves rather than a modification time. An\n// mtime has second or nanosecond granularity depending on the filesystem, and two\n// writes inside one tick would serve a stale graph; comparing the bytes cannot be\n// wrong. Reading still happens on every call, which is the 4% this deliberately\n// keeps.\n//\n// Every hit returns a defensive copy. Callers filter this graph in place — one of\n// them, `GetDeliveryIntegrity`'s own path filter, wrote through `Findings[:0]` — so\n// handing out the cached value would let the first caller truncate the graph for\n// every caller after it. That is the same aliasing defect `focusSurfaceGraph` had,\n// and a cache is exactly what turns it from a local surprise into a shared one.\nconst (\n\t// deliveryGraphCacheBytes and deliveryGraphOwnerBytes bound what the parsed graphs\n\t// retain, in estimated heap bytes (see estimateGraphBytes), globally and per\n\t// worktree. Alternating between projects or generations evicts the least recently\n\t// used graph instead of growing, and a graph larger than a budget is served\n\t// without being retained.\n\tdeliveryGraphCacheBytes = 192 << 20\n\tdeliveryGraphOwnerBytes = 96 << 20\n)\n\n// deliveryGraphCache holds parsed graphs keyed by the engine, the schema and the\n// digest of the bytes: identical bytes cannot produce a different graph, so two\n// worktrees that hold the same index share one entry and no worktree can be served\n// another's graph. The owner is the worktree root and only meters the budget.\nvar deliveryGraphCache = newBoundedCache(deliveryGraphCacheBytes, deliveryGraphOwnerBytes, 0, copyDeliveryIntegrityGraph)\n\n// DeliveryGraphCacheStats reports hits, coalesced waits, misses, evictions and the\n// bytes retained by the parsed-graph cache (spec pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches R7).\nfunc DeliveryGraphCacheStats() CacheStats { return deliveryGraphCache.Stats() }\n\n// parseDeliveryIntegrityGraph is parseDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr for callers that\n// treat any failure as \"no graph\".\nfunc parseDeliveryIntegrityGraph(owner string, raw []byte) (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, bool) {\n\tgraph, err := parseDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(owner, raw)\n\treturn graph, err == nil\n}\n\n// parseDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr returns the graph for these bytes, parsing only\n// when no graph for them is retained and, when several callers miss together, once.\n// owner is the worktree root the bytes were read from. When the cache has no room\n// for another production or waiter the error wraps ErrCacheBusy: overload is an\n// error the caller sees, never an unaccounted production outside the cache.\nfunc parseDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(owner string, raw []byte) (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, error) {\n\tsum := sha256.Sum256(raw)\n\tkey := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s|%d|%x\", engineVersion, len(raw), sum)\n\treturn deliveryGraphCache.Get(context.Background(), owner, key, graphParseReserve(raw), func() (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, int64, error) {\n\t\tparsed, ok := decodeDeliveryIntegrityIndex(raw)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, 0, errInvalidDeliveryIndex\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn parsed, estimateGraphBytes(parsed), nil\n\t})\n}\n\n// viewDeliveryIntegrityGraph hands fn the retained graph without copying it. fn\n// must neither modify nor keep the graph or anything it reaches; the callers that\n// only read a few fields of a large graph (a digest, a lookup) use it, the others\n// get a deep copy from parseDeliveryIntegrityGraph.\nfunc viewDeliveryIntegrityGraph(owner string, raw []byte, fn func(DeliveryIntegrityGraph)) bool {\n\treturn viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(owner, raw, fn) == nil\n}\n\nfunc viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(owner string, raw []byte, fn func(DeliveryIntegrityGraph)) error {\n\tsum := sha256.Sum256(raw)\n\tkey := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s|%d|%x\", engineVersion, len(raw), sum)\n\treturn deliveryGraphCache.View(context.Background(), owner, key, graphParseReserve(raw), func() (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, int64, error) {\n\t\tparsed, ok := decodeDeliveryIntegrityIndex(raw)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, 0, errInvalidDeliveryIndex\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn parsed, estimateGraphBytes(parsed), nil\n\t}, fn)\n}\n\n// ViewDeliveryIntegrity gives fn the delivery graph of the current index without a\n// copy (spec pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches R1). fn is read-only: it must not\n// modify or retain the graph. The error is that of GetDeliveryIntegrity.\nfunc (s Store) ViewDeliveryIntegrity(fn func(DeliveryIntegrityGraph)) error {\n\traw, _, err := s.ReadIndex(\"delivery-integrity.json\")\n\tif errors.Is(err, ErrIndexGenerationUnavailable) {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: delivery-integrity index not found; run `pose index`\")\n\t}\n\tif err := viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(s.Root, raw, fn); err != nil {\n\t\treturn deliveryIndexError(err)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nvar errInvalidDeliveryIndex = errors.New(\"pose: invalid delivery-integrity index\")\n\n// deliveryIndexError names an overload as such and anything else as an invalid index.\nfunc deliveryIndexError(err error) error {\n\tif errors.Is(err, ErrCacheBusy) {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: the delivery-integrity graph cache is busy: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\treturn errInvalidDeliveryIndex\n}\n\n// estimateGraphBytes estimates the heap a parsed graph retains by walking every\n// field of it (see estimateRetainedBytes), so no variable-sized field is charged a\n// constant. TestGraphByteEstimateTracksTheMeasuredHeap calibrates it on the real index.\nfunc estimateGraphBytes(g DeliveryIntegrityGraph) int64 { return estimateRetainedBytes(g) }\n\n// graphParseReserve is what a parse is expected to hold while it runs: the bytes\n// being decoded and the graph being built.\nfunc graphParseReserve(raw []byte) int64 { return int64(len(raw)) * 4 }\n\n// decodeDeliveryIntegrityIndex reads schema 1, written as the expanded graph, and\n// schema 2, which stores validation runs and sets once. Any other schema is not\n// read as either.\nfunc decodeDeliveryIntegrityIndex(raw []byte) (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, bool) {\n\tvar head struct {\n\t\tSchemaVersion int `json:\"schema_version\"`\n\t}\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &head); err != nil {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, false\n\t}\n\tswitch head.SchemaVersion {\n\tcase DeliveryIntegritySchemaVersion:\n\t\tvar graph DeliveryIntegrityGraph\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &graph); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, false\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn graph, true\n\tcase DeliveryIntegrityIndexSchemaVersion:\n\t\tvar compact compactDeliveryGraph\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &compact); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, false\n\t\t}\n\t\tgraph, err := expandDeliveryIndex(compact)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, false\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn graph, true\n\t}\n\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, false\n}\n\nfunc copyStringMap(in map[string]string) map[string]string {\n\tif in == nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tout := make(map[string]string, len(in))\n\tfor k, v := range in {\n\t\tout[k] = v\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\n// copyDeliveryIntegrityGraph copies every field that a caller could write through.\n// TestDeliveryGraphCopyCoversEveryField fails when a field is added to the struct\n// without being handled here, so an omission cannot become a silently shared slice.\nfunc copyDeliveryIntegrityGraph(in DeliveryIntegrityGraph) DeliveryIntegrityGraph {\n\tout := in\n\tout.Nodes = make([]DeliveryIntegrityNode, len(in.Nodes))\n\tfor i, node := range in.Nodes {\n\t\tnode.Attributes = copyStringMap(node.Attributes)\n\t\tout.Nodes[i] = node\n\t}\n\tout.Edges = append([]DeliveryIntegrityEdge{}, in.Edges...)\n\tout.Claims = append([]ArtifactClaim{}, in.Claims...)\n\tout.Findings = append([]DeliveryIntegrityFinding{}, in.Findings...)\n\tout.Deliveries = append([]DeliveryTarget{}, in.Deliveries...)\n\tout.ValidationResults = make([]DeliveryValidationResult, len(in.ValidationResults))\n\tfor i, result := range in.ValidationResults {\n\t\tresult.ScopeProvenance = copyStringMap(result.ScopeProvenance)\n\t\tresult.MaterialProvenance = copyStringMap(result.MaterialProvenance)\n\t\tout.ValidationResults[i] = result\n\t}\n\tout.RoadmapCriteria = make([]RoadmapCriterion, len(in.RoadmapCriteria))\n\tfor i, criterion := range in.RoadmapCriteria {\n\t\tcriterion.Refs = append([]string(nil), criterion.Refs...)\n\t\tcriterion.Reasons = append([]string(nil), criterion.Reasons...)\n\t\tout.RoadmapCriteria[i] = criterion\n\t}\n\tout.Archivals = append([]ArchivedFragment{}, in.Archivals...)\n\tout.Removals = make([]ArtifactRemoval, len(in.Removals))\n\tfor i, r := range in.Removals {\n\t\tif r.Supersedes != nil {\n\t\t\tsupersedes := make(map[string]string, len(r.Supersedes))\n\t\t\tfor k, v := range r.Supersedes {\n\t\t\t\tsupersedes[k] = v\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tr.Supersedes = supersedes\n\t\t}\n\t\tif r.Refusals != nil {\n\t\t\trefusals := make(map[string]string, len(r.Refusals))\n\t\t\tfor k, v := range r.Refusals {\n\t\t\t\trefusals[k] = v\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tr.Refusals = refusals\n\t\t}\n\t\tout.Removals[i] = r\n\t}\n\tif in.Removals == nil {\n\t\tout.Removals = nil\n\t}\n\n\t// ChangeSet carries slices of its own, so copying the outer slice is not enough.\n\tout.ChangeSets = make([]ChangeSet, len(in.ChangeSets))\n\tfor i, set := range in.ChangeSets {\n\t\tset.Commits = append([]string{}, set.Commits...)\n\t\tset.Paths = append([]ObservedPath{}, set.Paths...)\n\t\tif set.MaterialInputs != nil {\n\t\t\tinputs := MaterialInputs{Names: append([]string(nil), set.MaterialInputs.Names...), Moved: append([]string(nil), set.MaterialInputs.Moved...)}\n\t\t\tset.MaterialInputs = &inputs\n\t\t}\n\t\tout.ChangeSets[i] = set\n\t}\n\tout.Reverse = copyStringSliceMap(in.Reverse)\n\tout.Paths = copyStringSliceMap(in.Paths)\n\treturn out\n}\n\nfunc copyStringSliceMap(in map[string][]string) map[string][]string {\n\tif in == nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tout := make(map[string][]string, len(in))\n\tfor key, values := range in {\n\t\tout[key] = append([]string{}, values...)\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n//go:build !windows\n\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"runtime\"\n\t\"strconv\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"syscall\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// isolateProcessGroup starts the adapter in its own process group, and makes\n// the context's cancellation kill that whole group.\nfunc isolateProcessGroup(cmd *exec.Cmd) {\n\tcmd.SysProcAttr = &syscall.SysProcAttr{Setpgid: true}\n\tcmd.Cancel = func() error {\n\t\treturn syscall.Kill(-cmd.Process.Pid, syscall.SIGKILL)\n\t}\n}\n\n// killProcessGroup ends whatever the adapter left running.\nfunc killProcessGroup(cmd *exec.Cmd) error {\n\tif cmd.Process == nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\terr := syscall.Kill(-cmd.Process.Pid, syscall.SIGKILL)\n\tif errors.Is(err, syscall.ESRCH) {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn verifyReviewProcessGroup(cmd.Process.Pid)\n}\nfunc openReviewAdapterFile(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n\tfd, err := syscall.Open(path, syscall.O_RDONLY|syscall.O_NOFOLLOW|syscall.O_NONBLOCK, 0)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn os.NewFile(uintptr(fd), path), nil\n}\nfunc reviewSetupCommand(ctx context.Context, source *exec.Cmd) *exec.Cmd {\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, source.Path, source.Args[1:]...)\n\tcmd.Env = source.Env\n\tisolateProcessGroup(cmd)\n\tcmd.WaitDelay = time.Second\n\treturn cmd\n}\n\n// Verify the covered Linux group after SIGKILL. Zombies have terminated and\n// cannot hold pipes; reaping is their parent's duty. Other Unix ports currently\n// report signal delivery only and cannot require verified group termination.\nfunc verifyReviewProcessGroup(pid int) error {\n\tif runtime.GOOS != \"linux\" {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(time.Second)\n\tfor {\n\t\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(\"/proc\")\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process-group termination could not be observed\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(entries) > 100000 {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process inventory exceeds coverage\")\n\t\t}\n\t\talive := false\n\t\tfor _, entry := range entries {\n\t\t\tif _, err := strconv.Atoi(entry.Name()); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + entry.Name() + \"/stat\")\n\t\t\tif processGone(err) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process inventory incomplete\")\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tend := strings.LastIndex(string(raw), \")\")\n\t\t\tif end < 0 {\n\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process inventory malformed\")\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfields := strings.Fields(string(raw[end+1:]))\n\t\t\tif len(fields) < 3 {\n\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process inventory malformed\")\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tgroup, err := strconv.Atoi(fields[2])\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process group malformed\")\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif group == pid && fields[0] != \"Z\" && fields[0] != \"X\" {\n\t\t\t\talive = true\n\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !alive {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif time.Now().After(deadline) {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process group remained alive after termination\")\n\t\t}\n\t\ttime.Sleep(10 * time.Millisecond)\n\t}\n}\n\n// processGone reports the two ways a process exiting during the walk answers:\n// the open finds no entry, or the read of an open entry finds no process.\nfunc processGone(err error) bool {\n\treturn errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) || errors.Is(err, syscall.ESRCH)\n}\n\n// processGroupProbe keeps when the process inventory was last observed in\n// full. The probe asks whether this system lets the engine observe a process\n// group's termination; that does not change between two verifications of a\n// closeout, and the walk reads every /proc/<pid>/stat. A success holds for\n// processGroupProbeTTL; a failure is never kept (spec\n// pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place).\nvar processGroupProbe struct {\n\tsync.Mutex\n\tok time.Time\n}\n\nconst processGroupProbeTTL = time.Minute\n\n// processGroupVerify is the walk the probe runs; tests count it.\nvar processGroupVerify = verifyReviewProcessGroup\n\nfunc reviewProcessGroupAvailable() error {\n\tprocessGroupProbe.Lock()\n\tdefer processGroupProbe.Unlock()\n\tif !processGroupProbe.ok.IsZero() && time.Since(processGroupProbe.ok) < processGroupProbeTTL {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif err := processGroupVerify(-1); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tprocessGroupProbe.ok = time.Now()\n\treturn nil\n}\nfunc openReviewRootFile(root *os.Root, path string) (*os.File, error) {\n\treturn root.OpenFile(path, os.O_RDONLY|syscall.O_NOFOLLOW|syscall.O_NONBLOCK, 0)\n}\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\n// A diff that changes the review contract cannot be the authority that approves\n// itself. When the reviewed scope touches the review policy or a review profile,\n// the obligations are resolved a second time from the contract as it stood at the\n// change set's resolved base, and anything the diff weakened is restored for the\n// duration of this review. Adopting a weaker contract stays possible; approving\n// it under the weaker contract does not.\n//\n// Those two directories are the contract: the policy and the profiles carry their\n// own schema versions, so there is no separate schema path to guard. Rule bodies\n// under .pose/rules are deliberately outside this gate — a criterion names a rule,\n// and it is the criterion contract that is compared here.\n//\n// The restoration is not a second policy engine: it loads the same files through\n// the same parsers and composes them through the same selector and composer, at\n// a different revision. What it adds is direction — obligations may be restored\n// upward, never downward.\nconst (\n\treviewGovernancePolicyPrefix  = \".pose/policy/\"\n\treviewGovernanceProfilePrefix = \".pose/review-profiles/\"\n\n\t// A policy or profile is a small JSON document. The bound exists so a\n\t// crafted blob at the base revision cannot be read without limit.\n\treviewBaselineMaxBytes = 1 << 20\n)\n\n// ReviewPlanPolicyBaseline reports whether this plan was resolved under a\n// protected contract and what that protection changed.\ntype ReviewPlanPolicyBaseline struct {\n\tProtected bool `json:\"protected\"`\n\t// Reason is filled when the scope changes the contract but protection could\n\t// not be established. It is stated rather than silently skipped: an\n\t// unprotected governance review is the failure this field exists to expose.\n\tReason   string   `json:\"reason,omitempty\"`\n\tRevision string   `json:\"revision,omitempty\"`\n\tPaths    []string `json:\"paths,omitempty\"`\n\t// Weakened lists contract changes observed in the diff. Restored lists the\n\t// obligations this plan put back for the review of that diff.\n\tWeakened []string `json:\"weakened,omitempty\"`\n\tRestored []string `json:\"restored,omitempty\"`\n}\n\nfunc reviewGovernanceContractPath(path string) bool {\n\tclean := strings.TrimPrefix(filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path)), \"./\")\n\treturn strings.HasPrefix(clean, reviewGovernancePolicyPrefix) ||\n\t\tstrings.HasPrefix(clean, reviewGovernanceProfilePrefix)\n}\n\n// reviewGovernanceGuard is what the plan knows before it trusts the working\n// tree's policy: which contract paths this scope changes, and the revision whose\n// contract governed before it did.\ntype reviewGovernanceGuard struct {\n\tPaths    []string\n\tRevision string\n\tReason   string\n\t// Unreadable records that the working tree's own contract could not be read\n\t// for this scope, so the protected one governed in its place.\n\tUnreadable string\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) resolveReviewGovernanceGuard(scope ScopeRef) reviewGovernanceGuard {\n\tguard := reviewGovernanceGuard{}\n\tspecs, err := s.reviewScopeSpecs(scope)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn guard\n\t}\n\twanted := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, spec := range specs {\n\t\twanted[spec.Slug] = true\n\t\tclaims, found, claimErr := ParseArtifactClaims(spec, ArtifactPolicy{})\n\t\tif claimErr != nil || !found {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, claim := range claims {\n\t\t\tfor _, path := range []string{claim.Path, claim.OldPath, claim.NewPath} {\n\t\t\t\tif path != \"\" && reviewGovernanceContractPath(path) {\n\t\t\t\t\tguard.Paths = append(guard.Paths, filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path)))\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// The guard only reads the graph: it reads the retained one in place and\n\t// keeps nothing it reaches, only the strings it derives.\n\terr = s.viewDeliveryIntegrity(func(graph DeliveryIntegrityGraph) {\n\t\tsets := []*ChangeSet{}\n\t\tfor i := range graph.ChangeSets {\n\t\t\tif wanted[graph.ChangeSets[i].Spec] {\n\t\t\t\tsets = append(sets, &graph.ChangeSets[i])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tsort.Slice(sets, func(i, j int) bool { return sets[i].ID < sets[j].ID })\n\t\tfor _, set := range sets {\n\t\t\tfor _, observed := range set.Paths {\n\t\t\t\tfor _, path := range []string{observed.Path, observed.OldPath, observed.NewPath} {\n\t\t\t\t\tif path != \"\" && reviewGovernanceContractPath(path) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tguard.Paths = append(guard.Paths, filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path)))\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Mirror the review subject: the first attributed set, ordered by id,\n\t\t\t// supplies the base. A scope with several sets is reviewed against the\n\t\t\t// contract that governed before any of them landed.\n\t\t\tif guard.Revision == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tguard.Revision = set.ResolvedBase\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif len(guard.Paths) > 0 {\n\t\t\tguard.Reason = \"delivery-integrity index is unavailable, so no base revision is attributable; run `pose index`\"\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn guard\n\t}\n\tguard.Paths = uniqueSorted(guard.Paths)\n\tif len(guard.Paths) > 0 && guard.Revision == \"\" {\n\t\tguard.Reason = \"no attributed change set resolves a base revision for the contract change\"\n\t}\n\treturn guard\n}\n\n// reviewPolicyBaseline is the contract as it stood at a revision.\ntype reviewPolicyBaseline struct {\n\tRevision string\n\tPolicy   ReviewPolicy\n\tBase     ReviewProfile\n\tOverlays []ReviewProfile\n\tResolved bool\n}\n\n// reviewBaselineRevision refuses anything that is not an immutable object name\n// before it reaches a Git argument. The revision arrives from an index file in\n// the repository, so it is input: a value beginning with `-` would be read by\n// Git as an option rather than a revision, and a symbolic name would make the\n// protected contract depend on where a ref points today. The structural detector\n// already holds its subject to this shape; the baseline had not.\nfunc reviewBaselineRevision(revision string) error {\n\tif !designDeltaRevisionRE.MatchString(revision) {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: %q is not an immutable Git revision\", revision)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) parseReviewPolicyAt(revision string) (ReviewPolicy, error) {\n\tif err := reviewBaselineRevision(revision); err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewPolicy{}, err\n\t}\n\traw, err := gitShowBounded(s.Root, revision, \".pose/policy/review.json\", reviewBaselineMaxBytes)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewPolicy{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: reading review policy at %s: %w\", revision, err)\n\t}\n\treturn s.parseReviewPolicy(raw)\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) parseReviewProfileAt(revision, ref string) (ReviewProfile, error) {\n\tif err := reviewBaselineRevision(revision); err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewProfile{}, err\n\t}\n\tparts := strings.Split(ref, \"@\")\n\tif len(parts) != 2 || ValidateSlug(parts[0]) != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewProfile{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: invalid review profile ref %q\", ref)\n\t}\n\traw, err := gitShowBounded(s.Root, revision, \".pose/review-profiles/\"+parts[0]+\".json\", reviewBaselineMaxBytes)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewProfile{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: reading review profile %q at %s: %w\", ref, revision, err)\n\t}\n\treturn s.parseReviewProfile(ref, raw)\n}\n\n// resolveReviewPolicyBaseline reads the whole contract at one revision. A\n// baseline that cannot be read is reported, never assumed permissive.\nfunc (s Store) resolveReviewPolicyBaseline(revision string, scopeKind string) (reviewPolicyBaseline, error) {\n\tbaseline := reviewPolicyBaseline{Revision: revision}\n\tif IsGitEmptyTree(revision) {\n\t\t// Before a repository's first commit no contract governed anything.\n\t\tbaseline.Resolved = true\n\t\treturn baseline, nil\n\t}\n\tpolicy, err := s.parseReviewPolicyAt(revision)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn baseline, err\n\t}\n\tbaseline.Policy = policy\n\tif !policy.Enabled {\n\t\t// Nothing to protect: review was not governed at the base revision, so\n\t\t// the diff is not weakening a contract that existed.\n\t\tbaseline.Resolved = true\n\t\treturn baseline, nil\n\t}\n\tbaseRef := policy.Profiles[scopeKind]\n\tif baseRef == \"\" {\n\t\treturn baseline, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: baseline policy at %s configures no review profile for %s\", revision, scopeKind)\n\t}\n\n// viewDeliveryIntegrity hands fn the index's retained graph without copying\n// it, for callers that only read it: fn must neither modify nor keep the graph\n// or anything it reaches (spec pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place).\nfunc (s Store) viewDeliveryIntegrity(fn func(DeliveryIntegrityGraph)) error {\n\traw, _, err := s.ReadIndex(\"delivery-integrity.json\")\n\tif errors.Is(err, ErrIndexGenerationUnavailable) {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: delivery-integrity index not found; run `pose index`\")\n\t}\n\tif err := viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(s.Root, raw, fn); err != nil {\n\t\treturn deliveryIndexError(err)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) GetDeliveryIntegrity(path string) (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, error) {\n\traw, _, err := s.ReadIndex(\"delivery-integrity.json\")\n\tif errors.Is(err, ErrIndexGenerationUnavailable) {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, err\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: delivery-integrity index not found; run `pose index`\")\n\t}\n\tgraph, perr := parseDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(s.Root, raw)\n\tif perr != nil {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, deliveryIndexError(perr)\n\t}\n\tif path != \"\" {\n\t\tif err := ValidateArtifactPathIn(s.fsys(), s.Root, path, false); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\twanted := filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path))\n\t\tgraph.Reverse = map[string][]string{wanted: graph.Reverse[wanted]}\n\t\t// Allocates rather than filtering through the backing array: the graph\n\t\t// may come from the parse cache, and writing through it would narrow\n\t\t// every later caller's findings to this one path.\n\t\tfiltered := []DeliveryIntegrityFinding{}\n\t\tfor _, finding := range graph.Findings {\n\t\t\tif finding.Path == wanted {\n\t\t\t\tfiltered = append(filtered, finding)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tgraph.Findings = filtered\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'reviewProcessGroupAvailable|verifyReviewProcessGroup|processGroupVerify|resolveReviewerContainment' pose-mcp/internal/pose; cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md; rg -n 'review.guard|process.group|integrity.graph' .pose/knowledge --glob '*.md'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'reviewProcessGroupAvailable|verifyReviewProcessGroup|processGroupVerify|resolveReviewerContainment' pose-mcp/internal/pose; cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md; rg -n 'review.guard|process.group|integrity.graph' .pose/knowledge --glob '*.md'\"","aggregated_output":"pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:16:\toriginal := processGroupVerify\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:18:\t\tprocessGroupVerify = original\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:27:\tprocessGroupVerify = func(int) error {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:34:\tif reviewProcessGroupAvailable() == nil || reviewProcessGroupAvailable() == nil || probes != 2 {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:39:\t\tif err := reviewProcessGroupAvailable(); err != nil {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:49:\treviewProcessGroupAvailable()\npose-mcp/internal/pose/process_inventory_unix_test.go:74:\t\tif err := verifyReviewProcessGroup(-1); err != nil {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go:96:\tsafety, err := resolveReviewerContainment(a)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go:100:func resolveReviewerContainment(a ReviewerAdapter) (*ReviewExecutionSafety, error) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go:132:\t\tif err := reviewProcessGroupAvailable(); err == nil {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go:40:\treturn verifyReviewProcessGroup(cmd.Process.Pid)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go:60:func verifyReviewProcessGroup(pid int) error {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go:131:// processGroupVerify is the walk the probe runs; tests count it.\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go:132:var processGroupVerify = verifyReviewProcessGroup\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go:134:func reviewProcessGroupAvailable() error {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go:140:\tif err := processGroupVerify(-1); err != nil {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_windows.go:24:func reviewProcessGroupAvailable() error                              { return fmt.Errorf(\"process-group termination unsupported\") }\n# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Documentation Style\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for process documentation, rules, specs, workflows, and operational instructions.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Write instructions in the imperative mood and begin them with action verbs.\n- Keep bullets short and limited to one idea.\n- Avoid duplicating sections with the same purpose across files.\n- Link to the single source of truth instead of copying its content.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- State each instruction's scope explicitly to reduce ambiguity.\n\n## Examples: good and bad\n\n### Redundancy\n\n- **Good:** \"Update review criteria in `.pose/workflows/review.md` and reference that workflow from the root AGENTS file.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Repeat review criteria in AGENTS, the workflow, and every related spec.\"\n\n### Ambiguous reference\n\n- **Good:** \"Run the lint check described by the review workflow.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Run that standard validation before pushing.\"\n\n## Quick editorial checklist\n\n- Language is imperative and direct.\n- Bullets are short and non-overlapping.\n- Files do not duplicate sections.\n- `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` are used consistently.\n- References point to explicit files or paths.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md:27:over the whole delivery-integrity graph, not per-spec — see the\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:293:  the delivery-integrity graph's reverse lookups;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
